<?php
/**
 * Spartrak — the "sign out?" confirmation.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * WHY IT EXISTS
 * ===========================================================================
 * Signing out is one click away in the header chip, in the mobile drawer, in
 * the account sidebar and in Porto's own authorization link — and on a phone
 * those controls sit next to the ones a shopper actually meant to press. It
 * is also not free to undo: coming back means the phone number, the password
 * and, on a shared device, doing it again. So it gets a confirmation, in
 * Arabic and English, asked for on 2026-09-08.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * NOT A NEW COMPONENT — THE SIGNED-OUT PANEL WITH A QUESTION IN IT
 * ===========================================================================
 * This is the same shared frame + shared empty-state block that
 * html/signed-out-dialog.phtml uses (components/_dialog.less and
 * components/_empty-state.less own both), with three differences and no new
 * design language:
 *
 *   - the title asks instead of announcing;
 *   - there are TWO ways out instead of one, so `__copy`'s single-action
 *     column becomes `__actions`, a pair (see _empty-state.less);
 *   - NO `autoDismiss`. That option is explicitly only for a panel that
 *     announces something already done — see js/spartrak-dialog.js. A dialog
 *     that asks a question must never answer it by timing out.
 *
 * The illustration is the signed-out artwork, unchanged. It depicts exactly
 * what is about to happen, and inventing a second one for the question form of
 * the same event would be authoring an asset Figma does not contain (CLAUDE.md
 * section 3).
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * HOW IT INTERCEPTS EVERY SIGN-OUT CONTROL WITH NO TEMPLATE FORKS
 * ===========================================================================
 * `triggerSelector` claims every anchor pointing at the logout route,
 * wherever it was rendered — the two Spartrak templates, Magento's account
 * sidebar delegate and Porto's `.authorization-link` alike. The alternative
 * was forking a core template and a Porto template just to add a data
 * attribute, which CLAUDE.md section 2 rules out.
 *
 * `:not([href*="logoutSuccess"])` is not paranoia: the logout SUCCESS route
 * contains the logout route as a substring, so without it the confirmation
 * would also intercept a link to the post-logout page.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * IT CANNOT LOCK ANYONE OUT
 * ===========================================================================
 * Every trigger it intercepts is a REAL link to the real logout URL, and so is
 * the confirm button below. With this file's JS absent — or the dialog element
 * missing, or the module failing to load — nothing is prevented and the first
 * click signs the shopper out exactly as it did before. That is the whole
 * reason the confirm control is an <a href> and not a <button> with a script
 * behind it.
 *
 * ===========================================================================
 * COST
 * ===========================================================================
 * Rendered on every page for everyone, INCLUDING guests — and that is the
 * cheap answer, not the lazy one. This markup is inside the full-page cache,
 * so gating it on "is signed in" would fork a second cache entry of every page
 * in the store to save ~700 bytes, and a hole-punched block would cost a
 * request. A guest simply has no logout link for it to intercept, so it never
 * opens. Same decision, same reasoning, as html/signed-out-dialog.phtml
 * beside it.
 *
 * No extra request and no extra JS module — it shares the one the contact and
 * signed-out dialogs already use. The illustration is a CSS background inside
 * a subtree that is `display: none` until the dialog opens, so the file is not
 * fetched on a page where nobody signs out.
 *
 * @var \Magento\Framework\View\Element\Template $block
 * @var \Magento\Framework\Escaper $escaper
 */

$titleId = 'spartrak-logout-confirm-title';
$logoutUrl = $block->getUrl('customer/account/logout');
?>
<dialog id="spartrak-logout-confirm-dialog"
        class="spartrak-dialog"
        aria-labelledby="<?= $escaper->escapeHtmlAttr($titleId) ?>"
        data-mage-init='<?= $escaper->escapeHtmlAttr(json_encode([
            'spartrakDialog' => [
                'id' => 'logout-confirm',
                'triggerSelector' =>
                    'a[href*="customer/account/logout"]:not([href*="logoutSuccess"])',
            ],
        ])) ?>'>
    <div class="spartrak-dialog__panel">
        <div class="spartrak-dialog__frame">
            <div class="spartrak-empty-state spartrak-empty-state--panel spartrak-empty-state--lg">
                <span class="spartrak-empty-state__art spartrak-empty-state__art--signed-out" aria-hidden="true"></span>

                <div class="spartrak-empty-state__copy">
                    <strong class="spartrak-empty-state__title" id="<?= $escaper->escapeHtmlAttr($titleId) ?>">
                        <?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('Sign out of your account?')) ?>
                    </strong>

                    <p class="spartrak-empty-state__text">
                        <?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('You can sign back in at any time with your phone number.')) ?>
                    </p>

                    <?php /*
                      SAFE CHOICE FIRST, in DOM order — so it is what
                      showModal() autofocuses and what a keyboard reaches
                      first. A confirmation whose destructive button is
                      pre-focused answers itself on the next Enter press.
                    */ ?>
                    <div class="spartrak-empty-state__actions">
                        <button type="button"
                                class="spartrak-empty-state__action"
                                data-spartrak-dialog-close>
                            <?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('Stay signed in')) ?>
                        </button>

                        <a class="spartrak-empty-state__action spartrak-empty-state__action--primary"
                           href="<?= $escaper->escapeUrl($logoutUrl) ?>">
                            <?= $escaper->escapeHtml(__('Sign out')) ?>
                        </a>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</dialog>
